**Diary — 2026-02-11 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core CUDA engine over to HIP on an AMD Vega 10 / MI60-class GPU, target `gfx906`. The goal was not a full rewrite, just enough to get the existing CUDA kernels, memory model, and launch path running under ROCm/HIP.

Environment:

```text
GPU:      AMD Radeon Instinct MI60 / Vega 10, gfx906
Driver:   amdgpu
ROCm:     6.2
Compiler: hipcc
Target:   gfx906
```

The CUDA engine had roughly 18k lines of code, split into:

```text
engine_core.cu
engine_kernels.cu
engine_memory.cu
engine_streams.cu
engine_blas.cu
```

The first pass was mechanical: replace CUDA runtime calls with HIP equivalents.

```cpp
// CUDA
cudaMalloc(&d_x, bytes);
cudaMemcpy(d_x, h_x, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);
cudaMemset(d_x, 0, bytes);
cudaFree(d_x);

// HIP
hipMalloc(&d_x, bytes);
hipMemcpy(d_x, h_x, bytes, hipMemcpyHostToDevice);
hipMemset(d_x, 0, bytes);
hipFree(d_x);
```

Kernel syntax was mostly compatible:

```cpp
__global__ void scale_kernel(float* x, float* y, float a, int n) {
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (i < n) {
        y[i] = a * x[i];
    }
}
```

Launch syntax changed from:

```cpp
scale_kernel<<<grid, block, shared_bytes, stream>>>(x, y, a, n);
```

to:

```cpp
hipLaunchKernelGGL(scale_kernel, grid, block, shared_bytes, stream, x, y, a, n);
```

I kept the CUDA-style launch wrapper for a while:

```cpp
#define LAUNCH_KERNEL(kernel, grid, block, shared, stream, ...) \
